A||N||11||86SEFIERO||locking lugnuts need help||||1||
Z||000000||86SEFIERO||06-15-2000||03:52 AM||||<BR>the previous owner lost the tool to take off the locking lugnuts any suggestions on how I can get them off with out it?||||reg||1
Z||000001||RossT||06-15-2000||03:59 AM||||Check   <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/tt-lugpanic.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/tt-lugpanic.html</A> <P>This is an article from Ray Paulk.  Has everything you need to know..||||reg||1
Z||000002||86SEFIERO||06-15-2000||04:06 AM||||thanks for the info, but I have after market lugnuts made by gorrilla products they are round with half moon indentations in them Any other ideas? ||||reg||1
Z||000003||RossT||06-15-2000||04:08 AM||||you will most likely have to talk to the maker or a wheel shop that carries them..||||reg||1
Z||000004||phillip||06-15-2000||08:20 AM||||a couple of things i've done in the past with luglocks:<P>if you can find a socket size that almost fits you can go to a pawn shop or some place to get a bunch of cheap deep well sockets. hammer them on and try to take them off that way. had to do that with my '87 when i got it. haven't look at the '84 but it looks stock and i haven't pulled the covers off to see what i might have.<P>try using a cold chisel (i hope i'm calling it by the right name, i don't mean put it in the 'fridge). hammer it into one edge and try to knock it around till it's loose.<P>if this were the vette forum i'd probaly get some flaming for giving theives a method to steal from us (they're nice folks but some times a little paranoid if you ask me), but either method is a afternoon killer. "you goan git yo sweat on". unless you have to get them off for a flat or something i'd fight with a wheel or two a day.<P>hope this helps.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||06-15-2000||10:15 AM||||You may want to tape off the wheels to keep from gouging them during the,uh,operation.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||triker||06-15-2000||10:38 AM||||If you have access to a welder, just weld a 1/2 inch nut to each lug nut.||||reg||1
Z||000007||btoth||06-15-2000||10:44 AM||||side question:   does GM still carry the key for the factory locking lug nuts?   (the tire place stole mine!!!  and they cant seem to find it. go fig. )||||reg||1
Z||000008||JohnnyK||06-15-2000||02:20 PM||||I've had this problem.. Didn't seem like a big problem at first, but man, they were on good.. What worked nice on one was getting a smaller imperial deep socket, sledging it on (real good, cause you usually score it so bad you don't get another shot) and then cranking it off... The other ones were not so easy.. SInce I had those damn pontiac rims, there wasn't alot of room.. I tried dremal, no go, impact, no go... What I did in the end was dremal (with a cut off tool) enough to get pieces of it flaring out.. Then I covered the wheel around it with sheet metal, then oxy-acetelyned it, then banged a smaller socket on, then cranked.. If your lucky, it will grab (mind you, mine were seized and had been sitting).. It's a two man operation, because I learned you have to be quick with the torching and nut banging..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ed||06-15-2000||09:41 PM||||I recommend the socket approach too.  I  had MagGuards on my car and the "keyed" part eventually rounded out.  The tire shop just put a socket on them and they came right off.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||06-16-2000||12:55 PM||||most wheel and tire shops have a kit of special removal tool, sort of like ezouts, they hammer them on and remove the lugs. Of course the lugs are destroyed and youve got to get new ones.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||sloGT||06-16-2000||10:59 PM||||Btoth,yes Pontiac still carries the part,I just had to buy one for my 87 it runs about $10. ||||reg||1
